    topic: "The asset management job",
    level: 1,
    type: "primer",
    q: "Primer: what an asset manager does after closing",
    a: "After closing, the asset manager is accountable for the investment's results. The acquisition model becomes a business plan, and each year the plan becomes an operating budget and a capital plan. The property manager runs the building and reports monthly; the asset manager reviews results against budget, reforecasts the year and approves leases, capex and major contracts. Each quarter the numbers feed investor reports, lender compliance and valuations. Behind every decision sits one question: would we still buy this asset at today's value?",
    why: "Business plan: the strategy and targets for an asset through its sale. Budget: next year's plan, month by month. Reforecast: the latest full-year estimate, actuals to date plus a revised outlook. Variance: actual minus budget, favorable if it raises NOI. KPI: a key performance indicator, such as occupancy or collections. NER: net effective rent, rent after concessions and leasing costs. TI/LC: tenant improvements and leasing commissions. Downtime: months a space sits empty between leases. Watchlist: assets needing extra attention. Forward IRR: the return from holding, starting at today's net sale value. BOV: a broker's opinion of value.",
