    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "How is a property's annual operating budget built and approved?",
    a: "The property manager drafts it bottom-up, usually in the fall: revenue lease by lease from the rent roll plus renewal, leasing and vacancy assumptions, each expense line from its own driver and a separate capital budget. The asset manager tests it against the business plan, this year's results and comparable buildings, negotiates changes and approves it before the year starts.",
    why: "The budget is the contract between owner and manager for the year. It sets the targets the manager is judged on, the spending it can make without asking and the NOI the owner reports to investors and lenders. Building revenue from actual leases, rather than growing last year's total, ties it to what's signed and what's at risk. Others often have a say: joint venture partners may hold approval rights, and a loan under cash management may require lender approval, so budget season has real deadlines.",
    trap: "Review the capital budget with the operating budget. The two interact: deferring a repair, or classifying it as capex, makes NOI look better without saving anything.",
