    module: "re-am",
    topic: "Challenging a draft budget",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "The property manager sends you next year's draft budget. What do you push back on?",
    a: "Test revenue first: every lease expiry needs a renew-or-vacate assumption, and new leasing needs realistic timing, rents and concessions. Then check the big expense lines against the trailing twelve months, comparable buildings and actual quotes for taxes and insurance. Look for sandbagging (soft revenue and padded expenses that make targets easy to beat) and for missing known costs.",
    why: "Managers are usually judged against the budget, so a cautious one is in their interest: beating it looks like good management. Owners need an honest one, because it drives distributions, covenant forecasts, valuations and investor guidance. So push on the assumptions that carry the most money: large expiries, vacancy, rent growth and the three biggest expense lines. Then look for what's missing, such as a tax reassessment, a scheduled roof or a tenant that has given notice. The goal is a budget with an even chance of being beaten.",
    trap: "Don't just raise the draft until it matches the business plan. A budget forced up to the plan stops being a forecast. Record the gap to the plan honestly, and close it with actions, not assumptions."
