    q: "Walk me through how you'd review a budget-vs-actual report.",
    a: "Start at NOI: how far off, and is it revenue or expenses? Then work down the lines with the biggest dollar variances, not the biggest percentages. For each, get the cause from the manager and classify it: timing that reverses later or permanent, controllable or not. Then turn it into action: what fixes the permanent variances, and what they mean for the full-year reforecast, covenants and distributions.",
    why: "Variance review is how an asset manager finds problems early. Many owners set a threshold, such as 5% and $10K on a line, above which the manager must explain in writing. A variance is favorable if it raises NOI, so higher revenue and lower expenses are both favorable. The classification drives the response: a timing variance needs only watching; a permanent controllable one needs a plan with the manager; a permanent uncontrollable one, like a tax increase, goes straight into the reforecast.",
