    why: "A net variance can look fine while its parts point in opposite directions, and each part needs a different fix. The likely story here is that the manager pushed rents above what the market would absorb and lost occupancy for it. Whether that trade pays depends on the numbers: here it's slightly negative even before the turnover and marketing costs of empty units. The standard split values the occupancy change at budgeted rent and the rent change at actual occupancy, so the two parts add exactly to the total.",
