    topic: "Reading the monthly package",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "The monthly property package lands in your inbox. What do you check first?",
    a: "Cash and collections first: did the rent actually come in, and is delinquency rising? Then leasing: occupancy, move-ins and move-outs, renewals and upcoming expiries. Then NOI against budget, starting with the biggest dollar variances and the manager's explanations. Finally the balance sheet (receivables, payables, bank balances) and anything unusual, such as capex overruns, legal claims or thinning covenant headroom.",
    why: "Start with what's hardest to disguise and quickest to go wrong. Accrual NOI can look fine while cash isn't arriving, and leasing activity drives next quarter's NOI, so both warn earlier than the income statement. Variances explain the past month. The balance sheet catches what the income statement hides: unpaid bills, growing receivables or a bank balance that doesn't match the reported cash flow. Reading in the same order every month makes anomalies stand out.",
    trap: "Make the package tie. The bank reconciliation should match the balance sheet, and rent billed less rent collected should explain the change in receivables. Numbers that don't reconcile are the first question, not the last."
