    topic: "Lease trade-outs",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "New apartment leases are signing 3% below the prior rent and renewals 5% above. Renewals are 55% of leases signed. What's the blended trade-out, and what does it tell you?",
    a: "About +1.4%: 45% × −3% plus 55% × +5%. It's the average change from the expiring rent to the new rent on leases signed, so it shows where in-place rents are heading before the rent roll does. Here it's a warning: new leases below expiring rents mean the market is softening, and renewal increases usually follow the market down.",
    why: "An apartment rent roll reprices about once a year, so trade-outs show, month by month, how in-place rent is moving. New leases reflect today's market; renewals reflect how much residents will pay to avoid moving, which is why they usually hold up longer. A wide gap can't last: push renewals too far above market and residents leave, turning renewals into vacancies that re-lease at the lower market rent. Watch trade-outs alongside occupancy, since rent can be bought with vacancy.",
    formula: "Trade-out = (new rent − prior rent) ÷ prior rent\nBlended = new-lease share × new-lease trade-out + renewal share × renewal trade-out",
    trap: "Compare effective rents, after concessions. A new lease at a higher face rent with a month free can be a negative trade-out."
