    module: "re-am",
    topic: "Evaluating a lease proposal",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "A broker brings you a lease proposal for vacant space. How do you evaluate it?",
    a: "Start with the economics: face rent, escalations, free rent, TI and commissions, which combine into net effective rent and the lease's present value. Compare them with the budget, recent deals and your valuation assumptions. Then the tenant's credit, the term and how the expiry fits the building's rollover. Finally the other terms: options, termination rights, expense caps, use and exclusives.",
    why: "A lease is an investment: you spend TI, commissions and free rent up front to buy a stream of rent, so judge it by what's left after the costs and how reliable the stream is. Headline rent says little on its own, since concessions can give back years of it. Credit and term decide what the stream is worth, and timing matters too: a lease expiring alongside several others creates a rollover cliff. A termination option or a cap on recoveries can cost more than a dollar of rent.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what's the benchmark? Usually leasing guidelines approved with the budget, such as a minimum net effective rent and maximum concessions by space type. Deals inside them move fast; deals outside need the asset manager's approval, and big ones often the lender's."
