    topic: "Renewal vs new tenant",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "A tenant offers to renew for five years at $36/SF with $10 of TI. A new tenant would pay $40 after nine months of downtime, with six months free and $50 of TI. Which is better?",
    a: "The renewal, by a wide margin. Count the dark months as part of the new deal: renewing nets about $33/SF a year, replacing the tenant about $19, after downtime, free rent, a bigger TI package, full commissions and the operating costs you carry while the space is empty. The new tenant would need to pay about $58 to match.",
    why: "Losing a tenant is expensive in ways the face rent hides. Downtime brings no rent and no expense recoveries, while the empty space still costs taxes, insurance and utilities. New tenants also demand more free rent, bigger TI packages and full commissions, while a renewing tenant has its own moving costs and usually settles for less. So an incumbent at a rent somewhat below market is often the better deal, which is why landlords start renewal talks early. The exception is when you need the space back.",
    example: "Renewal: [[$36]] × 5 = $180, less [[$10]] of TI and [[$5]] of commissions: $165 over 5 years, $33.00 a year. New tenant: [[$40]] × 5 = $200, less $20 of free rent, [[$50]] of TI, [[$12]] of commissions and $9 of operating costs ([[$12]] a year for [[9]] months): $109 over 5.75 years, $18.96 a year.",
    trap: "Weigh the odds too: the new-tenant case assumes someone signs within nine months, and in a soft market it could take much longer. Discounting widens the renewal's lead, because its rent starts now."
