    topic: "Casualty events",
    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "A fire closes a third of your office building. What do you do as the asset manager?",
    a: "Make the building safe, stop further damage and notify the insurer, lender and tenants at once. Document the loss and file the claim, including loss-of-rents cover. Check each affected lease's casualty clause for rent abatement and termination rights. Then agree the rebuild plan with the insurer and the lender, which often controls the proceeds.",
    why: "A casualty tests the insurance program you bought at renewal. Property cover pays to repair or rebuild; loss-of-rents cover replaces the rent tenants stop paying while their space is unusable, which is why lenders require it. Speed matters because leases tie tenant rights to the restoration timeline: affected tenants usually stop paying rent, and a long rebuild can let them terminate. Loan documents usually let the lender hold the insurance proceeds and release them as work is done, or apply them to the loan if the damage is severe.",
    example: "Tenants in the damaged third pay [[$2.4M]] a year. Loss of rents cover runs [[12]] months, but the rebuild takes [[15]]. The policy replaces $2.4M of rent; the owner carries the last 3 months, $600K, plus the deductible, and more if tenants use their termination rights.",
    trap: "Find the gaps early: the deductible, any coinsurance shortfall, code upgrades the policy may not cover and the length of loss-of-rents cover. If restoration outlasts that cover, you carry the lost rent yourself."
