    a: "Take the idea, not the terms. Extending removes the rollover risk at expiry, which usually costs far more than the $10/SF of rent you'd give up. But don't cut straight to market: counter with a true blend and extend, charging $41.11 for all nine years, which keeps the total rent you're owed while giving the tenant relief now.",
    why: "A big expiry hangs over the whole building's value: buyers and lenders price the downside long before the lease ends, so an extension removes a discount from the building, not just a rollover cost. The tenant knows this and is using its leverage early, while its above-market rent is its weak point. Your leverage is its moving cost and disruption. Compare the options on present value with your own rent forecast, and decide what you'd trade: rate, TI, free rent or term.",
    formula: "Blended rent = (years left × current rent + extension years × new rent) ÷ total years\n= (2 × $45 + 7 × $40) ÷ 9 = $41.11",
    example: "If the tenant leaves at expiry ([[40%]] odds), replacing it costs about [[$140]]/SF in downtime, free rent, TI and commissions; if it renews, about [[$16]]. Expected cost: 40% × $140 + 60% × $16 = $65.60/SF. Extending now costs $10 of rent plus the same [[$16]]: $26/SF. The extension saves about $40/SF before discounting.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what if rents are rising? Then locking in $40 for seven years gives up upside, and a vacancy would re-lease faster, which shrinks the expected rollover cost. Run the comparison with your forecast, not today's market."
