    q: "A $500K lighting and controls retrofit would cut operating costs by $100K a year in a 90%-leased office building on net leases. Is it worth it to the owner?",
    a: "Only if the leases let you recover it. Tenants reimburse operating expenses, so 90% of the saving flows to them; the owner keeps the vacant space's $10K a year, a 2% return. Many net leases let the landlord recover the amortized cost of capital that cuts operating costs. With that clause the return is about 8.8%, and tenants still save.",
    why: "Whoever pays the bills gets the saving. That's the split incentive in net-leased buildings: the owner funds the capital while tenants enjoy lower bills. The fix is in the lease: clauses allowing recovery of cost-saving capital, usually amortized over its useful life with interest and often capped at the savings achieved. Even without them, the project can pay indirectly, since a lower total occupancy cost helps leasing, renewals and rent. In gross leases the owner keeps the saving; in base-year leases it depends on where costs sit against the base year.",
    example: "Amortize [[$500K]] over [[10]] years at [[8%]]: $74.5K a year. Tenants pay their [[90%]] share, $67.1K, and the owner keeps the vacant share of the saving, $10K: $77.1K a year for 10 years, an 8.8% return. Tenants still save $22.9K a year net.",
    trap: "Read the major leases before promising a return. Recovery clauses vary: some exclude capital entirely, and some cap the charge at the savings actually achieved. One large tenant without the clause changes the math."
