    topic: "Property management agreements",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What are the key terms of a property management agreement?",
    a: "The scope and fee, usually a percentage of collected revenue with a minimum, plus any leasing and construction management fees. Which costs the property reimburses, especially on-site payroll, and what the manager can spend without approval. Reporting deadlines, cash controls, insurance and indemnities. And, most important for the owner, the term and termination rights.",
    why: "The agreement is the owner's main lever over a manager, so the terms that matter most constrain behavior and allow an exit. Spending limits stop budget creep: the manager can pay budgeted items and emergencies, but anything else above a threshold needs approval. Termination rights, ideally without cause on 30 to 90 days' notice and automatically on a sale, keep the manager responsive. Lenders usually require the agreement to be subordinate to the loan, so they can replace the manager after a default.",
    trap: "Watch affiliated managers. When the sponsor's own company manages the property, the fee and termination rights are a conflict of interest, so LPs often benchmark fees against the market and keep the right to remove the manager."
