    q: "When would you appeal a property's tax assessment, and how would you make the case?",
    a: "When the assessed value is above what the property would sell for, or above the assessments of similar properties. Check every assessment notice against your valuation and watch the filing deadline. Build the case on income (actual NOI and a market cap rate), comparable sales and comparable assessments. Many owners hire a tax consultant paid a share of the savings.",
    why: "Assessors value thousands of properties with mass-appraisal models, so individual buildings drift from market value, especially after vacancy rises, rents fall or a big tenant leaves. Appeals succeed on evidence. The income approach carries the most weight for income-producing property, and the assessor's own figures for similar buildings can show you're taxed unequally even if the value itself is defensible. Appeal windows are short, often weeks after the notice, so track them across the portfolio. A win lasts only until the next reassessment.",
    formula: "Supported value = NOI before taxes ÷ (cap rate + effective tax rate)\nAnnual saving = (assessed value − supported value) × tax rate",
