    q: "How do you decide whether to hold or sell an asset?",
    a: "Treat holding as buying the asset at today's price. Estimate what a sale would net now, after costs and debt, and project the forward IRR on that equity. Hold if it beats what you'd require to buy it today, or what the money could earn at similar risk elsewhere; otherwise sell. Then weigh fund life, taxes, debt terms and timing.",
    why: "What you paid is sunk; the only money at stake is what you could take out today. Since-inception returns describe the past and mislead in both directions: a deal with a great IRR to date may have little left to offer, while a disappointing one may have its best years ahead. The forward return prices the remaining upside against the risk of carrying it. Practical factors can override the math. A closed-end fund near its end must sell; prepayment costs or cheap in-place debt change the equity number; a taxable owner may prefer to defer a gain.",
    trap: "Don't compare the forward IRR with your original underwriting. A 9% forward return looks weak next to an 18% plan, but if new deals of similar risk offer 8%, holding is the better use of the money."
